sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock
================================

Sparky Stereo OS drives NVIDIA cards with its own build of NVIDIA's open
kernel modules, nvidia-kernel-open-dkms-stereo3d, which adds HDMI 3D modes
and HDMI deep colour. NVIDIA's own driver packages do not have them. NVIDIA's
CUDA repository publishes every new driver next to the CUDA toolkit, and the
edition's hardware setup adds that repository at priority 1002, above the
edition's own 1001. Without this package, the next driver NVIDIA publishes
would replace the stereo module with NVIDIA's at the first upgrade.

/etc/apt/preferences.d/10sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock is generated from
this package's version when it is built. Its four records, in order:

1. NVIDIA's and Debian's own NVIDIA kernel module packages
   (nvidia-kernel-open-dkms, nvidia-kernel-dkms, nvidia-open-kernel-dkms):
   priority -1, never installed. The stereo module provides
   nvidia-kernel-open-dkms at NVIDIA's version, so NVIDIA's userspace is
   satisfied by it.
2. Every NVIDIA driver package at the version the stereo module is built
   for: priority 1005. The set is the one NVIDIA's own
   nvidia-driver-pinning-<version> package pins (the source packages
   nvidia-graphics-drivers, nvidia-open, nvidia-modprobe,
   nvidia-persistenced, nvidia-settings, nvidia-xconfig, libnvidia-nscq,
   libnvsdm, nvidia-fabricmanager, nvidia-imex, and cuda-drivers*), plus the
   stereo module itself. NVIDIA's own source nvidia-kmod-open is not named,
   because this lock package is built from a source of that name.
3. The same packages at any other version: priority 100, so they are never
   preferred while the locked version is available, whatever priority a
   repository is given.
4. This package itself: priority 1001 from the edition's repository (origin
   Sparky Stereo OS), so another preferences file that matches its name,
   for example a hand-made "*nvidia*" pin at an old version, can neither
   hold it back nor downgrade it and take the stereo module with it.

The version includes NVIDIA's Debian revision (for example 615.71.09-2), not
only the driver version: the stereo module provides nvidia-kernel-open-dkms
at exactly that revision, and NVIDIA's nvidia-driver depends on exactly that
revision. A rebuilt 615.71.09-3 from NVIDIA would otherwise be preferred.

The file name starts with "10" so that APT reads it before NVIDIA's own
pinning file (/etc/apt/preferences.d/nvidia-driver-pin): the first
version-specific record that matches decides a version's priority
(apt_preferences(5), "How APT Interprets Priorities").

CUDA toolkit packages (cuda-toolkit-*, cuda-cudart-* and the rest) are not
named here and keep updating. A CUDA release that needs a newer driver waits
until the stereo module moves to that driver.

Moving the driver to a new version
----------------------------------

1. Rebuild the stereo module from NVIDIA's DKMS tree of the new version with
   the patches rebased (the changelog version carries NVIDIA's version, for
   example 620.10.01-1+stereo3d1), and publish both binary packages.
2. The first upgrade installs the new lock only: the old lock still holds the
   driver, so nothing else moves.
3. The next upgrade moves the stereo module and NVIDIA's userspace together
   to the new version.

A lock inside the module package itself could never move: the old lock would
keep the old firmware-nvidia-gsp, which the new module depends on at exactly
the new version, so the new module would be held back for ever.

To leave the stereo module for NVIDIA's own driver, purge it and this package
("apt purge nvidia-kernel-open-dkms-stereo3d sparky-stereo-nvidia-driver-lock");
removing without purging keeps the configuration file, and with it the lock.
